WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:04.440
Weak passwords and poor recovery workflows make account takeover easier.

00:00:04.660 --> 00:00:08.660
Password policy, breached-password checks, reset flows,

00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:13.120
and administrative recovery processes all affect authentication strength.

00:00:13.340 --> 00:00:18.540
MFA failures occur when multifactor authentication is missing, optional, bypassable,

00:00:18.760 --> 00:00:22.600
or undermined by weak recovery and exception processes.

00:00:22.820 --> 00:00:27.220
Privileged access and sensitive tenant operations require particular attention.

00:00:27.440 --> 00:00:32.000
Session management determines whether authenticated access remains trustworthy after

00:00:32.220 --> 00:00:35.900
login. Tokens should be protected, rotated when appropriate,

00:00:36.120 --> 00:00:40.520
invalidated after logout or major changes, and resistant to replay.

00:00:40.740 --> 00:00:45.060
Credential attacks such as credential stuffing, password spraying,

00:00:45.280 --> 00:00:51.280
and brute force attempts require rate limits, lockout logic, monitoring, alerting,

00:00:51.500 --> 00:00:53.260
and risk-based checks.

00:00:53.480 --> 00:00:57.320
Use multi-factor authentication for privileged and sensitive access.

00:00:57.540 --> 00:01:01.140
Handle sessions with secure token practices and timeouts.

00:01:01.360 --> 00:01:04.800
Apply strong password policies and credential monitoring.

00:01:05.020 --> 00:01:11.020
Selected SOC 2 mappings for this topic include CC6.1 - Access controls;

00:01:11.240 --> 00:01:14.600
CC6.2 - Authentication mechanisms.
